# ---------------- HELP SHORT ----------------
#
# Syntax:
# - Everything written after a hash '#' will be ignored until the end of line.
# - Space and tabulation characters are ignored at beginning and end of each line.
# - Empty lines are ignored.
# - EVERY data value is given in the following format:
#   > An even number of lowercase hexadecimal digits.
#   > Each pair of hexadecimal digit corresponds to a byte.
# - .
#
# Data items:
# - User data items are stored EXLUSIVELY in the stack.
# - Their names are prefixed by underscores '_' according to their scope.
#   The deepest the scope is, the more it will have underscores in prefix.
#   Global scope data items starts with 1 underscore as prefix.
# - Data items are always refered using their name followed by an offset suffix.
#   An offset suffix is a plus sign '+' followed by 4 hex digits.
#   This way, you are refering to a "stack location".
#   This is the only way to interact with the stack.
#
# Registers:
# - Registers are some special memory locations you can use to read/write from.
# - There is a fixed amount of them, each with its specific use.
# - Every register is 4 or 8 bytes long, depending on the architecture targetted (64=8B).
#   Therefore, every operation made with them implies working with the whole memory chunk.
# - All the registers available here are:
#   > p1,p2,p3,p4,p5,p6 corresponds to function parameters.
#   > r                 corresponds to function return value.
#
# Instructions:
#   Stack:
#   - rsv = "reserve"
#     Making place in the stack for some bytes, now accessible via the data item name given.
#
#   - v2d = "value to data item write"
#     Write bytes given into a stack location.
#
#   - d2d = "data item to data item write"
#     Write from a stack location into another stack location, the given number of bytes.
#
#   - r2d = "register to data item write"
#     Write from a register into a stack location.
#
#   Registers:
#   - r2r = "register to register write"
#     Write from a register into a register.
#
#   - v2r = "value to register write"
#     Write bytes given into a register.
#
#   - d2r = "data item to register write"
#     Write from a stack location into a register.
#
#   Functions:
#   - fct = "function"
#     Declares a function invocation starting point.
#
#   - ivq = "invoque"
#     Pause current function execution, and move to the new function starting point given.
#
#   - bck = "back"
#     Stop current function execution, and resume it back, right after we were invoqued.
#
#   System:
#   - syc = "syscall"
#     Ask for a system call interrupt.
#
# --------------------------------------------
